1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,901
When Telok Ayer Street Was the Beach:

2
00:00:02,981 --> 00:00:05,800
Singapore's First Land Reclamations

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:10,500
When the Thian Hock Keng
temple was built on Telok Ayer

4
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:15,100
Street between 1839 and
1842, it stood on the beach.

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:18,991
Sailors landing from China
came up from the water to give

6
00:00:19,071 --> 00:00:23,250
thanks to Mazu, the goddess of
the sea, for a safe crossing.

7
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:27,171
By the end of the century it
was four streets from the sea,

8
00:00:27,251 --> 00:00:30,100
and today the water is further away still.

9
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:34,054
The land in front of it was
made by the colonial government,

10
00:00:34,134 --> 00:00:38,100
which levelled hills, built
seawalls and filled in the bay.

11
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:43,475
Singapore's reclamation began three
years after the British arrived.

12
00:00:43,475 --> 00:00:47,686
The south bank of the Singapore
River was low and swampy,

13
00:00:47,766 --> 00:00:52,053
and in 1822 Stamford Raffles
had a small hill near today's

14
00:00:52,133 --> 00:00:56,575
Battery Road levelled and its
earth used to fill the swamp.

15
00:00:56,575 --> 00:01:01,225
About 300 labourers did the
work in roughly four months.

16
00:01:01,225 --> 00:01:06,051
The new ground became Boat Quay along
the river and Commercial Square,

17
00:01:06,131 --> 00:01:09,775
later Raffles Place, the
centre of the town's trade.

18
00:01:09,775 --> 00:01:12,914
Along the coast to the
south, Telok Ayer Street,

19
00:01:12,994 --> 00:01:16,950
whose Malay name means "water
bay", followed the shoreline.

20
00:01:16,950 --> 00:01:21,390
The Nagore Dargah shrine,
built between 1828 and 1830,

21
00:01:21,470 --> 00:01:25,575
and the Al-Abrar Mosque,
which began as a thatched

22
00:01:25,655 --> 00:01:30,175
hut in 1827, faced the sea
along with Thian Hock Keng.

23
00:01:30,175 --> 00:01:34,204
The next stretch was the waterfront
east of Commercial Square,

24
00:01:34,284 --> 00:01:38,525
where the merchants' offices and
warehouses backed onto the sea.

25
00:01:38,525 --> 00:01:42,022
From the late 1850s a
seawall was built from Fort

26
00:01:42,102 --> 00:01:46,474
Fullerton south towards the
Telok Ayer Market, and the ground

27
00:01:46,554 --> 00:01:50,350
behind it was filled to
make a road along the water.

28
00:01:50,350 --> 00:01:55,075
The work was done by Indian
convict labour, and it went slowly.

29
00:01:55,075 --> 00:01:59,264
According to the National Library
Board's history of reclamation,

30
00:01:59,344 --> 00:02:02,875
the foundations could only
be laid at the lowest tides,

31
00:02:02,955 --> 00:02:05,108
which came about once a fortnight,

32
00:02:05,188 --> 00:02:08,800
so the wall took three years
and the road another year.

33
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:14,613
It was finished in 1864 and named Collyer
Quay after George Chancellor Collyer,

34
00:02:14,693 --> 00:02:18,125
the Chief Engineer of
the Straits Settlements.

35
00:02:18,125 --> 00:02:21,825
The largest project of
the century came next.

36
00:02:21,825 --> 00:02:26,277
Between 1879 and 1897 the Public
Works Department reclaimed

37
00:02:26,357 --> 00:02:30,656
a 42-acre strip of the bay in
front of Telok Ayer Street,

38
00:02:30,736 --> 00:02:34,957
using earth from Mount Wallich
and part of Mount Palmer,

39
00:02:35,037 --> 00:02:39,800
two hills to the south that were
blasted away for the purpose.

40
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:44,429
The first stretch, as far as Cecil
Street, was finished by 1886,

41
00:02:44,509 --> 00:02:48,622
and on the new land the
department laid out Cecil Street,

42
00:02:48,702 --> 00:02:52,675
Robinson Road and Raffles
Quay along the new seafront.

43
00:02:52,675 --> 00:02:55,498
The roads linked the commercial district

44
00:02:55,578 --> 00:02:57,900
with the docks at Tanjong Pagar.

45
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:01,400
In April 1881, the
Singapore Daily Times wrote

46
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:05,368
that beside the Telok Ayer
works, "the Collyer Quay

47
00:03:05,448 --> 00:03:08,950
reclamation sinks into
utter insignificance".

48
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:12,818
The old Telok Ayer Market,
which had stood on the

49
00:03:12,898 --> 00:03:16,847
shore at the end of Market
Street since the 1820s,

50
00:03:16,927 --> 00:03:20,875
was pulled down in 1879
as the reclamation began.

51
00:03:20,875 --> 00:03:25,065
Its replacement, designed
by the municipal engineer

52
00:03:25,145 --> 00:03:29,502
James MacRitchie with
cast-iron work made in Glasgow,

53
00:03:29,582 --> 00:03:33,600
was built on the new land
between 1890 and 1894.

54
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:37,500
That building survives
today as Lau Pa Sat.

55
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:42,651
In November 1896, at the request of
a member of the Legislative Council,

56
00:03:42,731 --> 00:03:45,049
the government published what the

57
00:03:45,129 --> 00:03:49,125
Telok Ayer reclamation had
cost and what it had earned.

58
00:03:49,125 --> 00:03:53,564
According to the statement, printed in the

59
00:03:53,644 --> 00:03:58,406
Singapore Free Press,
$917,243 had been spent

60
00:03:58,486 --> 00:04:02,065
on the work between 1879 and 1896,

61
00:04:02,145 --> 00:04:07,525
and it had produced 1,808,028
square feet of land.

62
00:04:07,525 --> 00:04:10,500
About 88,000 square feet had gone to

63
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:13,550
Finlayson Green and the new market.

64
00:04:13,550 --> 00:04:18,200
The government had sold
653,028 square feet at

65
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:23,958
an average of $1.13 a square
foot, bringing in $739,481,

66
00:04:24,038 --> 00:04:27,557
and more than a million square feet

67
00:04:27,637 --> 00:04:32,675
were still available for
sale or held in reserve.

68
00:04:32,675 --> 00:04:35,701
The statement put the
cost of reclamation at

69
00:04:35,781 --> 00:04:39,019
51 cents a square foot,
so the land was selling

70
00:04:39,099 --> 00:04:42,275
for more than twice what
it had cost to make.

71
00:04:42,275 --> 00:04:45,667
Even before the Telok
Ayer works were finished,

72
00:04:45,747 --> 00:04:48,850
a second, larger scheme began further out.

73
00:04:48,850 --> 00:04:52,835
Started in 1893, it was meant to reclaim

74
00:04:52,915 --> 00:04:56,675
88 acres behind a 5,000-foot seawall.

75
00:04:56,675 --> 00:05:01,553
In 1910 engineers found that
the seawall was sinking,

76
00:05:01,633 --> 00:05:06,230
and work was suspended
with 65 acres reclaimed and

77
00:05:06,310 --> 00:05:11,175
4,120 feet of wall built,
leaving a gap of 880 feet.

78
00:05:11,175 --> 00:05:14,633
The unfinished section
was turned into a tidal

79
00:05:14,713 --> 00:05:18,250
basin for small vessels,
the Telok Ayer Basin.

80
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:22,497
Work resumed in 1930 and
was completed in 1932,

81
00:05:22,577 --> 00:05:25,903
by which time the cost had risen from

82
00:05:25,983 --> 00:05:29,850
2.5 million to 15 million Straits dollars.

83
00:05:29,850 --> 00:05:32,658
A road built along the new land,

84
00:05:32,738 --> 00:05:36,800
Raffles Way, was renamed
Shenton Way in 1951.

85
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:40,315
According to the National
Library Board's account,

86
00:05:40,395 --> 00:05:43,335
all the reclamation of the colonial period

87
00:05:43,415 --> 00:05:46,650
added about 3 square
kilometres to Singapore.

88
00:05:46,650 --> 00:05:51,249
Between 1965 and 2015, the
country reclaimed about 138

89
00:05:51,329 --> 00:05:56,102
square kilometres more, at
Changi, along the East Coast,

90
00:05:56,182 --> 00:06:00,775
on Jurong Island, at Tuas
and around the river mouth.

91
00:06:00,775 --> 00:06:04,539
The Telok Ayer Basin itself was filled in

92
00:06:04,619 --> 00:06:08,476
between 1974 and 1977, and the land beyond

93
00:06:08,556 --> 00:06:12,025
the old waterfront became Marina Bay.

94
00:06:12,025 --> 00:06:16,358
According to the Singapore Land Authority,

95
00:06:16,438 --> 00:06:22,662
the country's land area grew
from 581.5 square kilometres in

96
00:06:22,742 --> 00:06:28,100
1960 to 744.3 square
kilometres at the end of 2025.

97
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:32,075
Two confirmed projects will
move the shoreline again.

98
00:06:32,075 --> 00:06:36,234
At Tuas, the Maritime and
Port Authority is building

99
00:06:36,314 --> 00:06:39,900
a new port in four
phases on reclaimed land.

100
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:44,509
The first phase of reclamation
was completed in 2021,

101
00:06:44,589 --> 00:06:48,932
and when all four phases
are finished in the 2040s

102
00:06:49,012 --> 00:06:53,445
the port will cover about
1,337 hectares and handle

103
00:06:53,525 --> 00:06:58,125
up to 65 million twenty-foot
container units a year.

104
00:06:58,125 --> 00:07:01,914
Off the East Coast, the Urban
Redevelopment Authority is

105
00:07:01,994 --> 00:07:06,543
planning "Long Island", which could
reclaim more than 800 hectares,

106
00:07:06,623 --> 00:07:08,961
about twice the size of Marina Bay,

107
00:07:09,041 --> 00:07:12,692
to protect the low-lying
coast from rising sea levels,

108
00:07:12,772 --> 00:07:17,125
create a new reservoir and provide
land for future development.

109
00:07:17,125 --> 00:07:20,718
As described when it was
announced in November 2023,

110
00:07:20,798 --> 00:07:25,874
the concept is three long tracts of
land from Marina East to Tanah Merah,

111
00:07:25,954 --> 00:07:29,829
with the new reservoir between
them and East Coast Park,

112
00:07:29,909 --> 00:07:34,500
and a tidal gate and pumping
station between each pair of tracts.

113
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:38,182
As of its update in March
2026, the plans were still

114
00:07:38,262 --> 00:07:41,293
being worked out through
technical studies,

115
00:07:41,373 --> 00:07:46,075
and reclamation will begin only
after those studies are complete.

116
00:07:46,075 --> 00:07:49,565
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Much of downtown

117
00:07:49,645 --> 00:07:53,075
Singapore stands on ground
that was once the bay.

118
00:07:53,075 --> 00:07:57,707
The shoreline has been moving since
1822, first with hills carried

119
00:07:57,787 --> 00:08:02,347
to the sea and seawalls built at
low tide, and the old waterfront

120
00:08:02,427 --> 00:08:07,425
streets like Telok Ayer Street still
trace where the beach used to be.
